{#
	The stat bar, shared by the home page and every category page.

	A macro rather than a copy in each: the two show the same figures over
	different sets, and a second copy is how one of them ends up a release
	behind. `stats` is whatever set is being summarized — `sites.stats` for the
	whole fleet, `group.stats` for one category.

	Everything here degrades on its own: the perfect-score trend only exists for
	the whole fleet, the Eleventy share only appears when there is one, and the
	Core Web Vitals tile only appears where CrUX has sampled anything. So a
	category page shows fewer tiles than the home page without the caller
	deciding which.
#}
{#
	Note the arguments. This is imported without `with context`, so the macro
	sees nothing of the page's own data — `meta` in particular is not in scope
	here, and reaching for it silently yields undefined rather than failing.
	Everything the bar needs is therefore passed in.
#}
{% macro statbar(stats, metrics, siteOfTheDay=null, screenshotService=null) %}
<section class="statbar">
	{#
		One of the perfect scores, held for the whole UTC day. Every eligible site
		is featured once before any is featured twice — see siteOfTheDay in
		lib/report.js. A picture rather than a row, because the leaderboard below
		already lists every one of them and the point of pulling one out is to
		show what it looks like.

		Cropped to the top of the page: at this size the whole screenshot would be
		a gray smudge, and the masthead is the recognizable part.
	#}
	{% if siteOfTheDay %}
	{% set sotd = siteOfTheDay.entry %}
	<div class="stat stat-sotd">
		<span class="stat-label">Perfect site of the day</span>
		{#
			Our own capture first: the same page load that produced the scores under
			it, taken at Lighthouse's own pixel ratio because this site scored a
			straight 400 — see isPerfectLighthouse in lib/runner.js, which exists so
			that the one screenshot shown this large is the sharp one.

			The service is the fallback, for a site that has not been re-measured
			since captures were introduced. Guarded on it being configured: with
			none, the filter returns an empty string, and `src=""` does not render
			nothing — it resolves to the current page and fetches this whole
			document as an image.
		#}
		{% if sotd.ownScreenshot %}
		<a class="sotd-shot" href="/site/{{ sotd.slug }}/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
			<img src="{{ sotd.ownScreenshot.src }}"
				width="{{ sotd.ownScreenshot.width }}" height="{{ sotd.ownScreenshot.height }}"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</a>
		{% elif screenshotService %}
		<a class="sotd-shot" href="/site/{{ sotd.slug }}/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
			<img src="{{ sotd.url | screenshot(screenshotService) }}" width="650" height="650"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</a>
		{% endif %}
		<p class="sotd-name"><a href="/site/{{ sotd.slug }}/">{{ sotd.displayUrl }}</a></p>
		<span class="rings">
			{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
			{% scoreRing sotd.latest.lab.scores[score.key], score.label %}
			{% endfor %}
			{% axeRing sotd.axe %}
			{% cwvRing sotd.cwvFailures, sotd.cwv.assessed, sotd.cwvWorst %}
		</span>
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{% if stats.recentlyMeasured.length or stats.newlyPerfect.length %}
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			What the measurement loop has just done. Everything else on this bar is
			a state of the corpus; this is the only thing that shows it moving.

			Gives up its rows to the newly-perfect list below when that one is long,
			and drops out entirely rather than printing a heading over nothing — so
			the card is gated on either list having something to say, not on this one.
		#}
		{% if stats.recentlyMeasured.length %}
		<span class="stat-label">Recently measured</span>
		<ol class="recents">
			{% for site in stats.recentlyMeasured %}
			<li>
				<a href="/site/{{ site.slug }}/">{{ site.displayUrl }}</a>
				{#
					The six rings reduced to their colors — the shape of that run at a
					glance, in the width a number would need. Decorative on purpose:
					the link beside it goes to the page with the actual figures, and
					six unlabeled colors read aloud would be noise.
				#}
				{#
					The span stays even when a run produced no bands at all. The row is
					a three-column grid, and dropping a child moves the age into the
					middle column, where the gap before the empty third one holds it
					half a rem off the right edge — so that row would sit out of line
					with the rest.
				#}
				<span class="recents-dots" aria-hidden="true">
					{% for band in site.bands %}<span class="recents-dot recents-dot-{{ band }}"></span>{% endfor %}
				</span>
				<span class="recents-age"><time datetime="{{ site.timestamp | iso }}">{{ site.timestamp | since }}</time></span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ol>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			The other half of the same story. The list above says what was measured;
			this says what changed — a site that was a point short and now is not.

			Every site that qualifies, however many that is — a site whose scores just
			went green should be able to find itself here, and a cap ordered by recency
			meant that on a busy day it silently missed the cut. On a quiet day this is
			a footnote under a list of twelve; on a busy one it is the whole card, and
			the list above stands down. Absent entirely where nothing has flipped, which
			is most category pages most days.
		#}
		{% if stats.newlyPerfect.length %}
		{#- Count and window in one pill: two chips said one thing twice, and the
		    window is what the count is counting over. The number comes from the
		    report so it cannot drift. -#}
		<span class="stat-label">Newly perfect <span class="tag stat-count" title="{{ stats.newlyPerfect | length }} {{ "site" if stats.newlyPerfect | length == 1 else "sites" }} reached perfect scores in the last {{ stats.newlyPerfectWindowHours }} hours">&times;{{ stats.newlyPerfect | length }} in {{ stats.newlyPerfectWindowHours }}h</span></span>
		<ol class="recents recents-flipped">
			{% for site in stats.newlyPerfect %}
			<li>
				<a href="/site/{{ site.slug }}/">{{ site.displayUrl }}</a>
				<span class="recents-dots" aria-hidden="true">
					{% for band in site.bands %}<span class="recents-dot recents-dot-{{ band }}"></span>{% endfor %}
				</span>
				<span class="recents-age"><time datetime="{{ site.timestamp | iso }}">{{ site.timestamp | since }}</time></span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ol>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{#
		Six medians, each computed on its own column — so this is a summary of the
		fleet rather than a portrait of any one site, and no site necessarily has
		this exact row.

		The Core Web Vitals ring rests on far fewer sites than the other five,
		since most of this corpus is too small for CrUX to sample. Its own tooltip
		says how many rather than letting the row imply they are all equally
		grounded.
	#}
	{#
		Two verdicts on the same fleet, in one card. They answer the same question
		from opposite ends — how many sites clear every bar, and how the rest
		divide on the one bar real users set — and reading them side by side is
		the point: 155 perfect against 1,261 CrUX has never heard of.
	#}
	{#
		Two thumbnails of the charts on /charts/, over this set's own sites.

		A macro because they are the same picture of different columns — no axes,
		no labels, no hit targets, none of which survive at 240px. The shape is the
		whole point: a smear that turns amber as it climbs, or one that falls away
		as it runs right.
	#}
	{#
		The two thumbnails share one card rather than taking a tile each.

		They are one thought — what a page's own weight costs it, and what other
		people's code costs it — and they link to the same place. Two tiles would
		have spent two of the bar's slots saying "there are charts over there".

		Each keeps its own heading, and they stack rather than sitting side by side
		— two 240px charts in a row need a card twice the width of every other tile
		on the bar, and one of them would have been the odd shape out.
	#}
	{#
		A banded distribution: one bar per verdict, over the sites that have one.

		Three of these now — performance, axe, Core Web Vitals — so it is a macro
		rather than a fourth copy of the same twenty lines. A bar may carry
		`segments`, in which case it is one column divided rather than several
		stacked beside each other; the segment heights are shares of that bar and
		the bar's height is its share of the tallest.
	#}
	{% macro tierbars(tiers, label) %}
	{% if tiers and tiers.rated %}
	<span class="stat-label">{{ label }} <span class="tag stat-count">&times;{{ tiers.rated | num }}</span></span>
	{% set tierMax = tiers.bars | maxValue("count") %}
	<span class="histogram histogram-tiers" role="img"
		aria-label="{{ label }} across {{ tiers.rated | num }} sites{% for b in tiers.bars %}. {{ b.tick }}, {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
		{% for b in tiers.bars %}
		<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.tick }} — {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural('site') }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
			<span class="histogram-track">
				<span class="histogram-bar histogram-bar-{{ b.key }}" style="height:{{ (b.count / tierMax * 100) | round(1) }}%">
					{%- for seg in b.segments %}
					<span class="histogram-seg histogram-bar-{{ seg.key }}" style="height:{{ seg.height }}%"
						title="{{ seg.label }}: {{ seg.count | num }} {{ seg.count | plural('site') }}"></span>
					{%- endfor %}
				</span>
			</span>
			<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
		</span>
		{% endfor %}
	</span>
	{% endif %}
	{% endmacro %}

	{% macro minichart(mini, label, anchor, xTitle, yTitle) %}
	<div class="stat-chart-item">
	<span class="stat-label stat-chart-label">{{ label }}</span>
	{#
		Straight to its own section rather than the top of the page. There are
		eight charts there, and landing on the first one leaves a reader who
		clicked the second thumbnail to go looking for what they just pointed at.
	#}
	<a class="stat-chart" href="/charts/#{{ anchor }}"
		aria-label="{{ mini.count | num }} {{ mini.count | plural('site') }} by {{ xTitle | lower }} and {{ yTitle | lower }} — see the full chart">
		<svg viewBox="0 0 {{ mini.w }} {{ mini.h }}" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
			{%- for band in ["poor", "average", "good"] %}
			<g class="plot-{{ band }}">
				{%- for dot in mini.dots[band] %}<circle cx="{{ dot.x }}" cy="{{ dot.y }}" r="1.6"/>{% endfor %}
			</g>
			{%- endfor %}
			{#
				Names only, no numbers: at this size tick values are illegible, but
				which scale is which still has to be said — the more so with two
				charts side by side whose vertical axes run opposite ways.
			#}
			<g class="mini-axis">
				<text x="{{ mini.axis.xLabel.x }}" y="{{ mini.axis.xLabel.y }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ xTitle }}</text>
				<text x="{{ mini.axis.yLabel.x }}" y="{{ mini.axis.yLabel.y }}" text-anchor="middle"
					transform="rotate(-90 {{ mini.axis.yLabel.x }} {{ mini.axis.yLabel.y }})">{{ yTitle }}</text>
			</g>
		</svg>
	</a>
	</div>
	{% endmacro %}

	{% if stats.scatterPreview or stats.thirdPartyPreview %}
	<div class="stat stat-plot">
		<div class="stat-charts">
			{% if stats.scatterPreview %}{{ minichart(stats.scatterPreview, "Page weight vs LCP", "weight-lcp", "Page Weight", "LCP") }}{% endif %}
			{% if stats.thirdPartyPreview %}{{ minichart(stats.thirdPartyPreview, "Third party vs performance", "third-party", "Third Party", "Score") }}{% endif %}
		</div>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			Label and figure on one line, the figure pushed to the right edge — the
			card carries four sections now, and a heading that owns a line of its
			own before its own number made the first of them the tallest.
		#}
		<span class="stat-headline stat-headline-inline">
			<span class="stat-label">Perfect {{ stats.perfect | plural("score") }}</span>
			{#
				How the count has moved. Every site, judged on its most recent
				measurement as of each day — the same rule as the number beside it,
				so the last point of the line is that number. Absent until there are
				enough days to be a trend — see MIN_HISTORY_DAYS in lib/report.js.

				Left of the number rather than right of it: the line ends where the
				figure begins, so the two read as one sentence about the same count
				rather than as a number with a decoration after it.
			#}
			{% if stats.perfectHistory.length %}
			{% set perfectTrend = { values: [] } %}
			{% for point in stats.perfectHistory %}
				{% set perfectTrend = (perfectTrend.values.push(point.perfect), perfectTrend) %}
			{% endfor %}
			<span class="stat-spark" title="Perfect scores from {{ stats.perfectHistory[0].date }} to {{ stats.perfectHistory[stats.perfectHistory.length - 1].date }}">
				{% sparkline perfectTrend, { width: 64, height: 14 } %}
			</span>
			{% endif %}
			<span class="stat-value">{{ stats.perfect | num }}<span class="stat-suffix">/{{ stats.measured | num }}</span></span>
		</span>
		{#
			What built them, rather than one figure for Eleventy and nothing about
			the rest. The rows sum to the number above — a site with no generator
			tag is counted under its own row instead of being dropped, which is
			what would make the two disagree.

			A presumption counts the same as a detection, as it does everywhere
			else on this site: most static sites emit no generator tag, so
			detections alone would credit the wrong thing.
		#}
		{% if stats.perfectGenerators.length %}
		{#
			The same pill list the Technology section uses, over the generators that
			produced a perfect score. A row of pills rather than a two-column table:
			this is three or four short names and their counts, which reads as a set
			rather than as a ranking, and it wraps where a table would not.
		#}
		<ul class="tally-list">
			{% for g in stats.perfectGenerators %}
			<li class="tally-item" title="{{ g.count | num }} of the {{ stats.perfect | num }} perfect {{ stats.perfect | plural('score') }} ({{ (g.count / stats.perfect * 100) | round }}%)">
				{% for mark in g.marks %}{% stackIcon mark, 14 %}{% endfor %}
				{#- No generator at all is the absence of a value, not a brand called
				    "None" — italics say so without a second column. #}
				<span class="tally-name">{% if g.id %}{{ g.name }}{% else %}<em>{{ g.name }}</em>{% endif %}</span>
				<span class="tally-count">{{ g.count | num }}</span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ul>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			Each Lighthouse circle as a distribution, banded the way the ring beside
			every row is banded — so a bar here cannot disagree with the circle it
			summarizes. Built in lib/report.js.
		#}

		{% if stats.measured %}
		<div class="ring-row">
			<span class="stat-label">Median Score <span class="tag stat-count">&times;{{ stats.medians.counts.scores | num }}</span></span>
			<span class="rings">
				{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
				{% scoreRing stats.medians.scores[score.key], "Median " + score.label %}
				{% endfor %}
				{% axeRing stats.medians.axe, 37, "Median axe violations: " + (stats.medians.axe | default("no data")) + ", across " + (stats.medians.counts.axe | num) + " sites" %}
				{% cwvRing stats.medians.cwvFailures, null, "poor", 37, "Median Core Web Vitals: " + (stats.medians.cwvFailures | default("no data")) + " failing, across " + (stats.medians.counts.cwv | num) + " sites with real-user data" %}
			</span>
		</div>

		{{ tierbars(stats.ringTiers, "Performance") }}
		{#
			"Axe", not "Accessibility". Lighthouse has an accessibility category of
			its own, and its ring is in the median row a few lines above — labelling
			this one "Accessibility" would put two different measurements under one
			word inside a single card. The scores are a sampled subset of the rules;
			this is the full violation count.
		#}
		{{ tierbars(stats.axeTiers, "Axe") }}

		{{ tierbars(stats.cwvVerdicts, "Core Web Vitals") }}
		{% endif %}
	</div>
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			A distribution rather than an average. The mean here is 1.11 MB and the
			median 256 kB — four times apart, because a handful of sites in the tens
			of megabytes drag the average somewhere almost no site actually sits. A
			single number could not say that; the shape does, at a glance.
		#}
		<span class="stat-label">Page weight</span>
		{% set weightMax = stats.weightBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="Page weight distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.weightBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.weightBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar" style="height:{{ (b.count / weightMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>
		{#
			JavaScript on its own, under the total. Its own bucket edges, because it
			is an order of magnitude smaller than the page around it — see
			SCRIPT_BUCKETS in lib/report.js — and its own leading bucket for the
			sites that ship none at all.
		#}
		{% if stats.scriptBuckets.length %}
		{% set scriptMax = stats.scriptBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="stat-label stat-label-sub">JavaScript Weight</span>
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="JavaScript weight distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.scriptBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.scriptBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar histogram-bar-script" style="height:{{ (b.count / scriptMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>

		{#
			Bytes from somebody else's domain, on the same scale as the two above it.
			The zero bucket is a real answer here — a third of these sites load
			nothing third-party at all — rather than a rounding of something small.
		#}
		<span class="stat-label stat-label-sub">Third Party Weight</span>
		{% set thirdPartyMax = stats.thirdPartyBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="Third party weight distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.thirdPartyBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.thirdPartyBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar histogram-bar-thirdparty" style="height:{{ (b.count / thirdPartyMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>
		{% endif %}
		{#
			Largest Contentful Paint, on the time scale rather than the byte one —
			its ladder carries 2.5s and 4s, Google's good and poor boundaries, so
			the thresholds land on the axis instead of inside a bar.
		#}
		{% if stats.lcpBuckets.length %}
		{% set lcpMax = stats.lcpBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="stat-label stat-label-sub">LCP</span>
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="Largest Contentful Paint distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.lcpBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.lcpBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar histogram-bar-lcp" style="height:{{ (b.count / lcpMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endmacro %}
